cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this note or its harness, cite it as below."
type: software
title: "Units before speed: what a dataflow-versus-von-Neumann benchmark actually measures, and what to report instead"
abstract: >-
  A measurement-methodology note on the benchmark published by kostyk348/tensor-broth. The audited
  table compares VM instructions per second with 27-tap multiply-accumulates per second and reads
  the ratio as a paradigm verdict; converted to a common unit (state updates per second) the
  advantage is 5.3-fold at the reported cube size and 14.4-fold at twice the edge length, while the
  choice of backend moves the same engine by a factor of 3.5 at the reported size. The note
  reproduces every configuration five times, reports the spread the single run hides, and lists
  what such a benchmark should print instead.
authors:
